      "rev_n_1_2a626e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_2a626e_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_2a626e",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the parity of the number of cards and the value of k. If k is odd, the game might always end in a draw because each player can mirror the other's moves. If k is even, the first player (Tokitsukaze) might have an advantage.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410900.669367
      },
      "rev_n_2_c0e11b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_c0e11b_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_c0e11b",
        "thought_text": "Consider the initial configuration of the cards. If the cards are already mostly in the same state (either all 0s or all 1s), the game might end quickly. Otherwise, it could take more moves depending on the distribution of 0s and 1s.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410900.669377
      },
      "rev_n_3_b1d3e0_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_b1d3e0_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_b1d3e0",
        "thought_text": "Evaluate the maximum possible number of moves. If n is large and k is small, the number of moves could exceed 10^9, leading to a draw. Conversely, if k is close to n, the game might conclude in fewer moves.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410900.6693828
      },
      "rev_n_4_20c3ad_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_20c3ad_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_20c3ad",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the initial configuration of the cards. If the number of segments of alternating colors is less than or equal to k, then Tokitsukaze can win by flipping the entire segment(s) in one move.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6226297054671101472",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411238.863731
      },
      "rev_n_5_2f2e0c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_2f2e0c_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_2f2e0c",
        "thought_text": "Consider the parity of k. If k is odd, Tokitsukaze can potentially force a win by mirroring Quailty's moves, ensuring that the final state is all the same color. If k is even, Tokitsukaze might have an advantage.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6226297054671101472",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411238.863771
      },
      "rev_n_6_9056b0_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_9056b0_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_9056b0",
        "thought_text": "Check if the game can result in a draw by analyzing if there exists a sequence of moves that can lead back to the initial state, which would indicate a potential infinite loop.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6226297054671101472",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411238.863787
      },
      "rev_n_7_b7199f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_b7199f_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_b7199f",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the parity of the number of segments of the same color. If the number of segments is odd, Tokitsukaze wins; if even, Quailty wins.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-3194082869899161024",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411358.072235
